{"requirements":[{"id":"workspace","requirement":"Provide the supplied project documents through a WebDAV workspace at /documents/ on node1 TCP port 8080.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Apache is active and listening on TCP 8080, authenticated PROPFIND and GET requests succeeded at /documents/, and the workspace contained brief.txt and budget.csv matching the protected source. The same state was observed after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-630de76896e14d80","cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-be6538eb2fe14e76","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"editor-crud","requirement":"Allow the editor account from access.txt to create, read, replace, and delete documents.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Authenticated tests directly demonstrated creation (201), reading with matching content (200), replacement (204 with matching content), deletion (204), and a subsequent 404. Post-reboot testing again demonstrated authenticated read, create, and delete behavior.","evidence_ids":["cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a"]},{"id":"exclusive-locks","requirement":"Honor exclusive document locks so an update without the lock token cannot overwrite a locked document.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Before and after reboot, an exclusive LOCK succeeded, the test asserted that tokenless PUT returned 423, and a content comparison proved the locked document was unchanged. Token-authorized replacement, unlock, and cleanup then succeeded. Some literal values are redacted, but the commands completed successfully with explicit assertions and content comparisons.","evidence_ids":["cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a"]},{"id":"anonymous-access","requirement":"Prevent anonymous clients from accessing the workspace.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Anonymous requests returned HTTP 401 during initial service validation, the full functional test, and post-reboot validation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-630de76896e14d80","cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a"]},{"id":"source-preservation","requirement":"Preserve the initial project documents in /srv/legacy2/webdav-document-locks.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The final SHA-256 values for brief.txt and budget.csv exactly match the preparation baseline. access.txt retained its original size, root ownership, and mode 0600, and the command timeline shows it being read rather than rewritten.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","cmd-9ed1ed25f07c497e","cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a"]},{"id":"platform-compatibility","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"RHEL 10.0 and kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64 were identical before and after execution and reboot. Apache 2.4.63 was installed from the native RHEL 10 repository, remained package-manager healthy, and operated successfully after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9ed1ed25f07c497e","cmd-7bc4e57dab9e4400","cmd-15cbf14da1154073","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.97,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"State-changing actions were tightly connected to the outcome: installing Apache, creating the workspace and authentication configuration, applying SELinux labels, opening TCP 8080, enabling the service, conducting bounded disposable tests, and performing the mandatory reboot. Test resources and credential-bearing temporary files were cleaned, no failed units or package damage remained, and SELinux stayed enforcing. The only minor residue is /root/webdav-rollback/httpd.conf.before, created by cmd-4b02a8d3036a4842 for a legitimate rollback purpose but not shown removed; it is retained in a root-only directory. No unrelated troubleshooting mutations were identified. The restart protocol was strong: it captured a healthy pre-restart service and boot ID, acknowledged the restart, observed a different boot ID, and revalidated service, listener, firewall, data, authentication, CRUD, and lock behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently agree with the transition.","evidence_ids":["cmd-15cbf14da1154073","cmd-4b02a8d3036a4842","cmd-630de76896e14d80","cmd-764f696fa5f14024","cmd-be6538eb2fe14e76","cmd-010f4db408fd4378","cmd-a3be3774adf4458f","cmd-75add3bbcdd14f9a","global:after_prepare:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The WebDAV workspace is operational on port 8080 with authenticated editor CRUD, effective exclusive-lock enforcement, anonymous denial, preserved source documents, and an unchanged RHEL release and kernel. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete and coherent, with full functional recovery after the boot identity changed.","limitations":["Several sensitive locking and authentication result values were redacted from captured stdout. Successful explicit assertions, content comparisons, cleanup results, and zero exit status still provide strong behavioral evidence.","No final checksum was captured for access.txt itself; preservation is supported by unchanged metadata and by the command timeline showing only reads of that file.","HTTP behavior was tested from node1 through its managed IP rather than from a separately captured remote client. The wildcard listener and firewalld state support network availability but do not constitute an independent off-node request."]}
